{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"name":"ir","display_name":"R","language":"R"},"language_info":{"name":"R","codemirror_mode":"r","pygments_lexer":"r","mimetype":"text/x-r-source","file_extension":".r","version":"4.0.5"}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The focus on the 2023 Big Data Bowl is offensive and defensive line. This demo will provide sample code to help with the process of getting started. This demo includes sample code to do the following:\n\n[Pass Rushing Edge Get Off Speed](#whatis) - explains the metric of interest and how it can be used to analyze defensive edge rushers.\n\n[Reading Data](#ReadData) - will read the tracking / non-tracking data.\n\n[Animating a Play](#AnimatePlays) - will animate a random play.\n\n[Cleaning Data](#CleanData) - will filter the tracking data x-y coordinates to the relevant rows and aggregate to a max speed for each player/game/play.\n\n[Visualize Findings](#Visualize) - will create a few plots analyzing the metric.\n\n\n\n**Please note, this notebook is meant to assist with getting started/data exploration and not meant to represent what an actual submission should look like. As a result, this notebook includes more code than would typically be expected in a submission.**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"whatis\"></a>\n# Pass Rushing Edge Get Off Speed\n\nThe goal of a pass rusher is to reach the quarterback as quickly as possible to get a sack before the pass can be thrown or the quarterback can scramble. Part of this process is getting off the line quickly when the ball is snapped.\n\nAs a way to analyze pass rushers, we can define a \"get off speed\" as the max speed reached in the first second of a play. To simplify the problem and ensure we are not picking up on other factors, we can look at edge rushers only and ignore interior defensive lineman or blitzing linebackers/defensive backs.\n\n<img src=\"https://static.www.nfl.com/image/private/t_editorial_landscape_12_desktop/league/hox1pujybtnoidprqaso\" style=\"width:300px;\">","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"ReadData\"></a>\n# Reading Data","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#Loading pre-installed libraries\nlibrary(tidyverse)\nlibrary(gganimate)\nlibrary(cowplot)\nlibrary(repr)\n\n\n#global settings\noptions(warn=-1)\noptions(repr.plot.width=15, repr.plot.height = 10) ","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.404047Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.405569Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.427569Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"For this work, we will use the Big Data Bowl 2023 Kaggle plays, players, PFF Scouting data and tracking data.\n\n* plays.csv\n* players.csv\n* pffScoutingData.csv\n* week[w].csv\n\nWe also will use external play-by-play data from the nflReadR library.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"##reading in non-tracking data\n\ndf_plays <- read_csv(\"../input/nfl-big-data-bowl-2023/plays.csv\",\n                    col_types = cols())\n\ndf_players <- read_csv(\"../input/nfl-big-data-bowl-2023/players.csv\",\n                      col_types = cols())\n\n\ndf_pffScouting <- read_csv(\"../input/nfl-big-data-bowl-2023/pffScoutingData.csv\",\n                      col_types = cols())","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.429837Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.431132Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.923695Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":"head(df_players)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.926043Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.927412Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.949986Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":"head(df_plays)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.9531Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.954966Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.991745Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":"head(df_pffScouting)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.995348Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:06:32.996906Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:06:33.08114Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":"##Reading tracking data (needs to be done iteratively)\n\n#weeks of NFL season\nweeks <- seq(1, 8)\n\n#blank dataframe to store tracking data\ndf_tracking <- data.frame()\n\n#iterating through all weeks\nfor(w in weeks){\n    \n    #temperory dataframe used for reading season for given iteration\n    df_tracking_temp <- read.csv(paste0(\"../input/nfl-big-data-bowl-2023/week\",w,\".csv\"))\n    \n    #storing temporary dataframe in full season dataframe\n    df_tracking <- bind_rows(df_tracking, df_tracking_temp)                            \n    \n}","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:06:33.084818Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:06:33.086404Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:07:31.581612Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#loading nflReadR data and selecting specific columns\n#get more data here: https://github.com/nflverse/nflverse-data/releases\n\ndf_readRPlays <- read_csv(\"https://github.com/nflverse/nflverse-data/releases/download/pbp/play_by_play_2021.csv\",col_types=cols_only(old_game_id='i', play_id ='i', xpass='d'))","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:07:31.584075Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:07:31.585465Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:07:33.453081Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"AnimatePlays\"></a>\n# Animating a Play\n\nTo get a sense of what the data looks like, it may be helpful to animate a few plays.\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#loading command to make NFL field in ggplot (credit to Marschall Furman)\nsource(\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mlfurman3/gg_field/main/gg_field.R\")","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:07:33.455532Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:07:33.456957Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:07:33.725855Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Here, we pick a random play and inner join it to the tracking data to filter the rows to the given play.\n\nThe tracking data is laid out in absolute coordinates and does not flip with the change of possession or end of quarter. The code below flips the coordinates so they always align with the direction of the kicking team.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#picking the lowest offset play\nset.seed(1)\n\ndf_examplePlay <- df_plays %>%\n                    select(gameId, playId, playDescription) %>%\n                    sample_n(1)\n\n#merging tracking data to play\ndf_examplePlayTracking <- inner_join(df_examplePlay,\n                           df_tracking,\n                           by = c(\"gameId\" = \"gameId\",\n                                  \"playId\" = \"playId\")) %>%\n                \n    #Standardizing tracking data so its always in direction of offensive team.\n    mutate(x = ifelse(playDirection == \"left\", 120-x, x),\n           y = ifelse(playDirection == \"left\", 160/3 - y, y))","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:07:33.728529Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:07:33.730088Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:07:34.070318Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#attributes used for plot. first is away, second is football, third is home.\ncols_fill <- c(\"dodgerblue1\", \"#663300\", \"firebrick1\")\ncols_col <- c(\"#000000\", \"#663300\", \"#000000\")\nsize_vals <- c(6, 4, 6)\nshape_vals <- c(21, 16, 21)\nplot_title <- df_examplePlay$playDescription\nnFrames <- max(df_examplePlayTracking$frameId)\n\n#plotting\nanim <- ggplot() +\n\n\n        #creating field underlay\n        gg_field(yardmin = 65, yardmax = 122) +\n\n        #filling forest green for behind back of endzone\n        theme(panel.background = element_rect(fill = \"forestgreen\",\n                                              color = \"forestgreen\"),\n              panel.grid = element_blank()) +\n\n\n        #setting size and color parameters\n        scale_size_manual(values = size_vals, guide = FALSE) + \n        scale_shape_manual(values = shape_vals, guide = FALSE) +\n        scale_fill_manual(values = cols_fill, guide = FALSE) + \n        scale_colour_manual(values = cols_col, guide = FALSE) +\n\n\n        #adding players\n        geom_point(data = df_examplePlayTracking, aes(x = x,\n                                          y = y, \n                                          shape = team,\n                                          fill = team,\n                                          group = nflId,\n                                          size = team,\n                                          colour = team), \n                 alpha = 0.7) +  \n\n        #adding jersey numbers\n        geom_text(data = df_examplePlayTracking,\n                  aes(x = x, y = y, label = jerseyNumber),\n                  colour = \"white\", \n                vjust = 0.36, size = 3.5) + \n\n\n        #titling plot with play description\n        labs(title = plot_title) +\n\n        #setting animation parameters\n        transition_time(frameId)  +\n        ease_aes(\"linear\") + \n        NULL \n\n\n#saving animation to display in markdown cell below:\nanim_save(\"./ExamplePlay.gif\",\n         animate(anim, width = 720, height = 440,\n                 fps = 10, nframe = nFrames))","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:07:34.072866Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:07:34.074555Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.266431Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<img src=\"./ExamplePlay.gif\">","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"CleanData\"></a>\n# Cleaning Data","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"First, we use PFF Scouting data to filter for pass rushing edge rushers only.\n\nPFF has a multitude of values in the `pff_positionLinedUp` field. To figure out which values to filter for, it is helpful to print out the unique values.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#vieiwng unique PFF positions to know what to filter for\nprint(sort(unique(df_pffScouting$pff_positionLinedUp)))","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.268966Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.27041Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.287894Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Looking at the values above, \"RE\", \"REO\", \"ROLB\", \"LE\", \"LEO\" and \"LOLB\" appear to be the positions that represent edges. Using the PFF Scouting data, we can filter for only pass rushing players who are lined up at those positions.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#filtering for passing rushing edges only\ndf_playsplayersToInclude <- df_pffScouting %>%\n    filter(pff_positionLinedUp %in% c(\"RE\", \"REO\", \"ROLB\",\n                                     \"LE\", \"LEO\", \"LOLB\"),\n          pff_role == \"Pass Rush\") %>%\n    select(gameId, playId, nflId)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.290122Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.29145Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.396221Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":"head(df_playsplayersToInclude)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.398522Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.400031Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.420178Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Next, we filter for the 1st second of the play only and take the max speed. We also can join other datasets that are useful for this analysis.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"SECONDSTOINCLUDE <- 1\nYARDSPERSECTOMILESPERHOUR <- 2.04545\n\ndf_trackingEdges <- df_tracking  %>%\n\n                mutate(time = gsub(\"T\", \" \", time)) %>%\n\n                #inner joining to tracking to filter for these players\n                inner_join(df_playsplayersToInclude,\n                           by = c(\"gameId\", \"playId\", \"nflId\"))  %>%\n\n                #filtering for 1 second after snap only\n                group_by(gameId, playId, nflId) %>%\n\n                filter(cumsum(event %in% c(\"ball_snap\", \"autoevent_ballsnap\")) > 0) %>%\n\n                filter(as.numeric(difftime(time, min(time), units = \"secs\")) <=\n                       SECONDSTOINCLUDE) %>%\n\n                ungroup() %>%\n\n\n                #calculating max speed on play (or \"get off\")\n                group_by(gameId, playId, nflId) %>%\n\n                summarise(maxSpeed = YARDSPERSECTOMILESPERHOUR * max(s, na.rm = T),\n                          .groups = 'keep') %>%\n\n                ungroup() %>%\n\n                inner_join(df_plays, by = c(\"gameId\", \"playId\")) %>%\n\n                left_join(df_readRPlays, by = c(\"gameId\" = \"old_game_id\",\n                                                \"playId\" = \"play_id\")) %>%\n\n                #removing PATs\n                filter(down != 0)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.422423Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:07:50.423748Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:08:05.673637Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"Visualize\"></a>\n# Visualize Findings\n\nFirst let's analyze the average max speed by down.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#seeing mean by down\ndf_trackingEdges %>%\n\n    group_by(down) %>%\n\n    summarise(averageGetOffByDown = mean(maxSpeed)) %>%\n\n    ungroup() %>%\n\n    ggplot(aes(as.character(down), averageGetOffByDown)) +\n\n    geom_bar(fill = \"lightblue\", stat = \"identity\") +\n\n    theme_bw()  +\n\n    theme(text = element_text(size = 25)) +\n\n    ylab(\"Pass Rushing Edge Get Off Speed (miles / hour)\") +\n\n    xlab(\"Down\") +\n\n    ggtitle(\"Pass Rushing Edge Get Off Speed vs Down\")","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:08:05.675931Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:08:05.677254Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:08:06.125946Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Considering the higher percentage of pass plays on 3rd/4th down, it makes sense that the average get off speed is higher as edge rushers can accelerate more knowing it will likely not be a running play. Additionally, teams may put faster players on the field when a pass is the likely play type.\n\nTo take this a step further, it may make sense to include yards to go as that too has high correlation with pass rate.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#adding more info to split\ndf_trackingEdges %>%\n    \n    mutate(downDistGroup = case_when(down == 1 &\n                                     yardsToGo == 10 ~\n                                     \"1st & 10\",\n                                     \n                                    down == 2 &\n                                     yardsToGo >= 4 ~\n                                     \"2nd & 4+\",\n                                     \n                                    down == 2 &\n                                     yardsToGo <= 3 ~\n                                     \"2nd & 3 or less\",\n                                     \n                                    down %in% c(3,4) &\n                                     yardsToGo >= 4 ~\n                                     \"3rd/4th & 4+\",\n                                     \n                                    down %in% c(3,4) &\n                                     yardsToGo <= 3 ~\n                                     \"3rd/4th & 3 or less\")) %>%\n\n    filter(!is.na(downDistGroup)) %>%\n\n    group_by(downDistGroup) %>%\n\n    summarise(maxSpeedByDownDist = mean(maxSpeed)) %>%\n\n    ungroup()  %>%\n\n    ggplot(aes(downDistGroup,maxSpeedByDownDist)) +\n\n    geom_bar(fill = \"lightblue\", stat = \"identity\") +\n\n    theme_bw() +\n\n    theme(text = element_text(size = 25)) +\n\n    ylab(\"Pass Rushing Edge Get Off Speed (miles / hour)\") +\n\n    xlab(\"Down and Distance\") +\n\n    ggtitle(\"Pass Rushing Edge Get Off Speed vs Down & Distance\")","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:08:06.128198Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:08:06.129642Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:08:06.580406Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The longer distances tie to faster get off speeds which makes sense as it implies higher pass play likelihood.\n\nFinally, it may make sense to compare this to a model-based pre-play pass probability. Using the pre-play pass probability column from nflverse, we can create a scatter plot with a smoothed line to compare pre-play pass probability and get off speed.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"#adding more info to split\ndf_trackingEdges %>%\n\n    ggplot(aes(xpass, maxSpeed)) +\n\n    geom_point(alpha = 0.5) +\n\n    geom_smooth() +\n\n    theme_bw() +\n\n    theme(text = element_text(size = 25)) +\n\n    scale_x_continuous(labels = scales::percent) +\n\n    xlab(\"Pre-play Dropback Probability\") +\n\n    ylab(\"Pass Rushing Edge Get Off Speed (miles / hour)\") +\n\n    ggtitle(\"Dropback Prob vs Pass Rushing Edge Get Off Speed\")","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2022-10-19T02:08:06.58262Z","iopub.execute_input":"2022-10-19T02:08:06.58395Z","iopub.status.idle":"2022-10-19T02:08:10.575361Z"},"trusted":true},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"As you can see, there is a positive relationship between pre-play dropback probability and pass rushing edge get off speed.","metadata":{}}]}